{% if OBJSTORAGE_BACKEND == "azure" and OBJSTORAGE_AZURE_ACKNOWLEDGE_RISK %}
# rclone S3 gateway. Two backends share this one hostname: GET/HEAD
# requests on the public bucket's path go to the unauthenticated,
# read-only, --include-scoped rclonegw-public; everything else (including
# signed requests against private buckets) goes to the authenticated
# rclonegw. See local-docker-compose-services for why this two-process
# split exists at all: rclone's `serve s3` has no bucket-level ACL/policy
# mechanism, unlike RustFS/MinIO.
#
# This uses `reverse_proxy` directly rather than Tutor's own `import
# proxy "host:port"` snippet. That snippet bundles a `log` directive,
# which Caddy rejects inside a `handle` block ("directive 'log' is not
# an ordered HTTP handler") — confirmed by actually starting this stack,
# not caught by any offline test. `handle`'s first-match-wins semantics
# are exactly what the public/private split needs, so logging and
# compression are hoisted to the site level instead, applying to both
# backends uniformly rather than duplicated per branch.
#
# CORS. Confirmed live, against the real rclonegw container: rclone's
# `serve s3` already sends a complete, correct set of Access-Control-*
# headers on its own for an actual GET/HEAD/PUT/etc. response - no
# `--allow-origin` flag needed, contrary to what an earlier version of
# this comment assumed. Caddy's own `header` directive, at the site or
# `handle` level, must NOT also inject a copy of any header rclone
# already sets: it does not replace what `reverse_proxy` later copies
# from the upstream response, it adds a second value - a real browser
# rejects a GET with two "Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *" lines just as
# hard as it rejects a response with none ("contains multiple values
# '*, *', but only one is allowed" - confirmed live, this used to be
# exactly what this Caddyfile produced). This is why Allow-Origin/
# -Methods/-Headers are set only inside the OPTIONS-only handle block
# below, never at the site level where they'd collide with rclone's own
# output on a real GET/HEAD/PUT.
#
# Access-Control-Expose-Headers is a narrower case of the same trap:
# rclone's own native value is just "ETag" (confirmed live), not the
# "Content-Range,Accept-Ranges,ETag" this backend's Range-request/video-
# scrubbing support needs exposed to JS. A site-level `header` can't fix
# this either - same duplication mechanism - so each `reverse_proxy`
# block below uses `header_down`, which operates on the upstream
# response rclone already wrote and genuinely replaces it (confirmed
# live: `header_down -Field` immediately followed by `header_down Field
# value` left the header missing entirely - a real Caddy quirk, not a
# theory - whereas a bare `header_down Field value` on its own replaces
# cleanly, exactly once).
#
# OPTIONS is a different, separate problem: confirmed live, `rclonegw`'s
# own OPTIONS handling is never preflight-safe. Against the bare path
# (no query string) it answers a plain `400 Bad Request`; against a
# presigned URL's exact query string - what a real browser preflight
# actually requests, since the preflight target matches the real
# request's URL - it answers `403 Forbidden`, because a presigned URL's
# signature is computed for the one method it was minted for (GET here)
# and `rclonegw` validates that signature regardless of method. A
# preflight response must be 2xx or the browser fails the whole request
# before ever sending the real one ("doesn't pass access control check:
# It does not have HTTP ok status"). Fixed by answering every OPTIONS
# request directly at Caddy, before either rclone gateway ever sees it.
#
# The discovery-bucket clause in the path matcher is conditional on
# purpose. OBJSTORAGE_DISCOVERY_BUCKET_NAME renders to an empty string when
# the `discovery` plugin is disabled, and an *unguarded* second path token
# would collapse this matcher to a bare "/*" — routing every bucket,
# including grades and ORA2 uploads, to the unauthenticated backend. Do
# not remove the conditional guard below, and see test_patches.py for the
# regression test that renders this with discovery disabled.
{{ OBJSTORAGE_AZURE_HOST }}{$default_site_port} {
    log {
        output stdout
        format filter {
            wrap json
            fields {
                common_log delete
                request>headers delete
                resp_headers delete
                tls delete
            }
        }
    }
    encode gzip

    @cors_preflight {
        method OPTIONS
    }
    handle @cors_preflight {
        header Access-Control-Allow-Origin *
        header Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, HEAD, PUT, POST, DELETE, OPTIONS"
        header Access-Control-Allow-Headers *
        header Access-Control-Max-Age 86400
        respond 204
    }

    @public_read {
        method GET HEAD
        path /{{ OBJSTORAGE_BUCKET_NAME }}/*{% if OBJSTORAGE_DISCOVERY_BUCKET_NAME %} /{{ OBJSTORAGE_DISCOVERY_BUCKET_NAME }}/*{% endif %}
    }
    handle @public_read {
        reverse_proxy rclonegw-public:9000 {
            header_up X-Forwarded-Port 80
            header_down Access-Control-Expose-Headers "Content-Range,Accept-Ranges,ETag"
        }
    }
    handle {
        reverse_proxy rclonegw:9000 {
            header_up X-Forwarded-Port 80
            header_down Access-Control-Expose-Headers "Content-Range,Accept-Ranges,ETag"
        }
    }
}
{% endif %}
